{"format_version":"vbulletin-3+-thread","url":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/promethean-the-created-aa/9054-my-friend-and-i-s-old-online-promethean-game","id":9054,"title":"My friend and I's old online Promethean game","path":["Home","Onyx Path","White Wolf Worlds","Chronicles of Darkness","Promethean: The Created"],"posts":[{"date":"2013-11-12T12:22:30-08:00","id":9054,"title":"My friend and I's old online Promethean game","content":"<span style=\"color:#404040\"><span style=\"font-family:Verdana\"><span style=\"font-size:11px\">My friend Lostkith and I played an online Promethean game back in 2011. Rain was a character created by Lostkith for a tabletop </span></span></span><span style=\"color:#404040\"><span style=\"font-family:Verdana\"><span style=\"font-size:11px\">Old West</span></span></span><span style=\"color:#404040\"><span style=\"font-family:Verdana\"><span style=\"font-size:11px\"> game that I ran. I thought it would be fun to put up for memory's sake.</span></span></span> <br /> <span style=\"color:#404040\"><span style=\"font-family:Verdana\"><span style=\"font-size:11px\"> It was March 19th, 1952. Fate had brought Rain to Winooski, Vermont. The American Woolen Company used the city\u2019s rivers to make their goods, just as the Burlington Mill Company had before them in the last century. Rain wondered how the American Woolen Company would fare after the Korean War ended, when their lucrative contracts would dry up. Experience, and the business skill he had learned so long ago watching Jason, told him that neither Winooski or the American Woolen Company had much of a future.<br /> <br /> Still, on that cold Wednesday, Winooski had a measure of prosperity. The hardships of the Great Depression and the second World War were receding for most to the realm of memory for most of the country. The bundled people that Rain passed all seemed happy. Only an old man with long white hair seemed to take any notice of him. He stopped shoveling the sidewalk in front of his home, and focused his full attention on Rain.<br /> <br /> The old man took a pipe out his coat pocket and lit it. \u201cSon, I don\u2019t think you should be here.\u201d<br /> <br /> The man\u2019s pipe captured Rain\u2019s attention. It looked exactly like a pipe he had seen almost seventy-five years earlier, in New Orleans. The passage of years had done nothing to dull the memory of that visit to New Orleans, and his adventure to find Leone, a missing French woman. The pipe he remembered had belonged to Roscoe Fox, a murderous gambler. Could it be the same pipe?<br /> <br /> The old man caught Rain\u2019s gaze, and the hostility evident only a moment before drained away. \u201cLike my pipe? Me too. I\u2019m told it belonged to a gambler from the Old West. But I can\u2018t vouch for the truth of that.\u201d<br /> <br /> So it was the same pipe. Memories of New Orleans in 1875 washed over him. It seemed like it was yesterday, but so much had happened since then. With a start Rain realized that the Twentieth Century was half over. Where did the time go?<br /> <br /> Perhaps to fill the silence, the old man said, \u201cA lady friend gave it to me after the first World War. Don\u2019t ask her name. You see, she was married, and I don\u2019t want to blemish her memory. I\u2019m sure you understand.\u201d <br /> <br /> Rain looks at the rising smoke from the pipe as a shadow of a smile passes across his face. \u201cI would like to take a closer look at that pipe if you don\u2019t mind. I would like to see if that is the same pipe I saw or one that is mate to it. That is if you don\u2019t mind parting with it for but a moment.\u201d A shiver courses over the old man as Rain taps into his reserve of pyros and pushes himself halfway into Twilight and scrutinizes the pipe as he hold out his hand. <br /> <br /> The old man handed over the pipe. As Rain takes it, he chuckles. \u201cBut I\u2019m sure it must be a mate to the one you saw. I\u2019ve had this pipe for the better than thirty years.\u201d<br /> <br /> Nevertheless, it became quite clear to Rain at once that it is the same pipe. Able to see into Twilight, he spots the shade of Roscoe Fox glowering at him. A gunshot wound still marred the center of his forehead, attesting to how the gambler died so many years ago. Rain could still remember what had happened.<br /> <br /> His search for Leone had taken him and Jacques, the girl\u2019s brother, to the Harpy\u2019s Tavern. They had learned that a vile gambler by the name of Roscoe Fox had apparently obtained for himself a new French wife. The description they had received matched Leone perfectly.<br /> <br /> After searching several dens of vice, they found Roscoe Fox playing cards with a trio of rough-looking characters. Behind him stood a woman, but not Leone. A mulatto beauty, the woman wore a red dress and had on an impressive feathered hat. In time, Rain would learn this woman was named Allie Knapp, and she would wind up changing his life forever.<br /> <br /> It was then that Rain noticed the other ghost by Roscoe Fox. Seeing her, Rain\u2019s thoughts returned to the present. The shade was a blonde woman with a fine figure and emerald green eyes that must have shone with great luster in life. She wore a green dress that looked right for the 1930s. Could this be the ghost of the woman who had given the old man the pipe?<br /> <br /> Rain looks into the old man\u2019s eyes and pushes his will hard and deep into his mind. He pulls with all his unformed soul\u2019s might upon the man\u2019s memory of the pipe and his ownership of it and steals it away from him, A shiver runs through Rain\u2019s body as his imperfections come to light for a brief moment or two as the power of the Pros and Azoth ripple across his body. The dazed expression in the old man\u2019s eyes quickly turns into one of fear and revulsion as Rain\u2019s Disquiet slams home into the mortal. With a look of regret and old hurt, Rain turns and leaves the bewildered, dazed, and staggering man to his disorientation. Rain\u2019s features ripple and change into the form of a younger looking blonde man with a far more slender looking build as he turns the corner out of sight of the old man, the resulting use of power sets a few passersby ill at ease, but none the wiser to what has just transpired. In his brisk walk away from the former pipe owner, Rain double checks to make sure the shades of the departed are indeed anchored to the pipe or if the ghost of the woman is more attached to the aged man. After noting this, Rain drops the use of his Bestowment and begins to lose himself within the mist of memories past as he continues to walk along, gently caressing the worn pipe as he does.<br /> <br /> The ghost of Roscoe Fox chases after his pipe, the one thing that still holds him to the mortal world. The shade of the blonde woman gives the old man a regretful kiss he cannot feel, and follows after Fox. She too is connected to the pipe, but not so much as the dead gambler. For his part, the old man returned to his house, the chore of shoveling forgotten.<br /> <br /> Rain walks, and remembers. He and Jacques had found Roscoe Fox, but he had another woman with him. Before the two could devise their next move, the mulatto beauty with Fox spotted them. Rain saw that something like recognition flash in her brown eyes at the sight of Jacques. She nodded toward the bar. She leaned over, whispered something in Fox\u2019s ear, and gracefully made her way over to the bar.<br /> <br /> There she met them. After ordering a bottle of whisky, she whispered to Jacques, \u201cShe\u2019s aboard the Terrifying Deborah, bound for a brothel in Cuba. You don\u2019t have much time. I\u2019ll get him drunk in case you want some revenge later.\u201d<br /> <br /> The woman received her drink, and turned away without another word. Rain and the brother rushed out into the chaos of New Orleans, determined to find the Terrifying Deborah before it was too late. While he did not yet know her name, Rain would meet Allie Knapp again.<br /> <br /> While Rain remembered, a police patrol car passed by him on the street. Seeing his chance to recover his pipe, Roscoe Fox took hold of the police officer driving the car. Commanding his body, Fox had the officer pull over and get out of the car.<br /> <br /> With a hand not his own, Fox drew a gun on Rain. With a voice not his own, Fox said, \u201cHand over the pipe, maggot!\u201d Alarmed onlookers scattered.<br /> <br /> Rain slowly backs away from the officer towards the wall of the building near him, all the while tightening his grip on the pipe. \u201cOK, Roscoe, I know that it must be you in there and I want you to know I have no compunctions about breaking the pipe and releasing your pitiful soul to whatever lays beyond the land of shadows that you inhabit. But the lady I do have a slight hesitation about, so I\u2019m going to give you a moment or two to this through\u2026 release the officer or I break the pipe!\u201d<br /> <br /> With a steely look in his eyes, Rain waits for the ghost\u2019s next move.<br /> <br /> The police officer blinks, a look of befuddlement on his face. The police officer - a man named Miller, going by his name tag - lowers his weapon. His voice shaking, he exclaimed, \u201cAs you all were.\u201d<br /> <br /> Miller hurried back to his car, and soon pulled away. For a bit, Rain kept his grip on the pipe, ready for any treachery. He knew from experience how tricky Roscoe Fox could be. His mind drifted back to their previous encounter in New Orleans.<br /> <br /> He and Jacques searched urgently for the Terrifying Deborah, determined to reach it before it set sail for Cuba. To quicken the search, Rain had called upon Eclipse, his faithful crow companion. With Eclipse\u2019s help, they found the ship they sought in short order. However, it quickly became clear that Roscoe Fox\u2019s female companion had tricked them. The vessel had gaping holes in its hull, and workmen swarmed over the damaged ship like ants under the supervision of circling seagulls and pelicans. It would be weeks before the Terrifying Deborah would be sailing anywhere.<br /> <br /> Grimly, Rain and the brother set back for the Harpy\u2019s Tavern, determined to gain real answers for Roscoe Fox and his lady friend.<br /> <br /> In the present, Rain relaxed, confident he had thwarted Roscoe Fox\u2019s ghost, for the time being at least. Cautiously, he resumed his walk. After some time and searching, he reached the address he sought. Rain studied the humble two-story white house. So this was where Mabel Irvine lived now.<br /> <br /> Rain made his way toward the house. After all, seeing Mabel Irvine was why Rain had come to Winooski, Vermont in the first place. <br /> </span></span></span><br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/790-cire","name":"Cire","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"549"}]},"thankedby":[]}]}